1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:01,776
Fifty thousand times a second,

2
00:00:01,948 --> 00:00:05,169
a machine fires a laser at a
falling drop of molten tin,

3
00:00:05,342 --> 00:00:07,010
and your phone depends on it.

4
00:00:07,081 --> 00:00:10,695
It sounds like a stunt, or a
bad joke told by a physicist,

5
00:00:10,876 --> 00:00:14,791
but it is hard to find a manufacturing
process that matters more.

6
00:00:14,899 --> 00:00:18,730
Inside every modern chip are
enormous numbers of tiny switches,

7
00:00:18,913 --> 00:00:22,439
and each one has to be drawn
with extraordinary precision.

8
00:00:22,588 --> 00:00:24,127
The drawing is done with light,

9
00:00:24,276 --> 00:00:26,708
and light has a problem:
it is a blunt pencil.

10
00:00:26,813 --> 00:00:30,548
For decades the whole industry has
been fighting that single fact,

11
00:00:30,717 --> 00:00:33,263
by making the pencil
sharper again and again.

12
00:00:33,353 --> 00:00:35,136
This is the story of the latest,

13
00:00:35,303 --> 00:00:39,203
strangest sharpening, and of the
one machine that makes it possible.

14
00:00:39,305 --> 00:00:41,062
Here is the strange thing about it:

15
00:00:41,263 --> 00:00:43,722
you will almost certainly
never see this machine,

16
00:00:43,873 --> 00:00:46,785
and most people could not name
the company that builds it.

17
00:00:46,878 --> 00:00:49,712
It is called extreme
ultraviolet lithography,

18
00:00:49,901 --> 00:00:53,868
usually shortened to EUV, and
it took decades to get working.

19
00:00:53,951 --> 00:00:56,343
To see why anyone would
go to such lengths,

20
00:00:56,509 --> 00:00:59,291
we first need to see how
a chip is actually drawn.

21
00:00:59,369 --> 00:01:01,915
A chip starts as a
polished disc of silicon,

22
00:01:02,088 --> 00:01:05,559
coated with a thin layer of a
material that reacts to light.

23
00:01:05,651 --> 00:01:09,730
A pattern is then shone onto that
coating through a stencil called a mask,

24
00:01:09,895 --> 00:01:12,761
and the pattern is shrunk
by optics on its way down.

25
00:01:12,872 --> 00:01:15,138
Where the light lands,
the coating changes,

26
00:01:15,289 --> 00:01:18,462
and the chip maker can etch the
shape into the silicon beneath.

27
00:01:18,568 --> 00:01:20,236
Then the disc is coated again,

28
00:01:20,403 --> 00:01:22,070
and the whole process repeats,

29
00:01:22,237 --> 00:01:26,518
layer after layer, until the wiring of
a processor is built up like a city.

30
00:01:26,632 --> 00:01:31,075
The size of the smallest line you can draw
depends on the wavelength of the light,

31
00:01:31,237 --> 00:01:33,892
the distance between one
wave crest and the next.

32
00:01:33,992 --> 00:01:37,942
Long waves smear detail the way a
thick marker smears a signature,

33
00:01:38,122 --> 00:01:42,012
so to draw finer lines, engineers
kept moving to shorter waves.

34
00:01:42,089 --> 00:01:45,274
The answer this machine uses
is a very short wavelength,

35
00:01:45,445 --> 00:01:47,208
thirteen and a half nanometres,

36
00:01:47,379 --> 00:01:49,939
which is the extreme
ultraviolet of the name.

37
00:01:50,066 --> 00:01:53,202
That one decision, to move
to such a short wavelength,

38
00:01:53,370 --> 00:01:56,786
turned a hard problem into one
that looked almost impossible.

39
00:01:56,873 --> 00:01:59,200
A shorter wavelength means a finer pencil,

40
00:01:59,366 --> 00:02:01,970
which is the entire
promise of this technology,

41
00:02:02,136 --> 00:02:05,073
and the rest of this film
is the price of keeping it.

42
00:02:05,191 --> 00:02:07,801
The idea is older than most people expect.

43
00:02:07,902 --> 00:02:11,825
The EUV story begins in the middle
of the nineteen-eighties in Japan,

44
00:02:11,998 --> 00:02:16,382
in a small research effort that most
of the industry did not take seriously.

45
00:02:16,468 --> 00:02:20,070
Nobody could be sure the physics
would ever become a factory tool,

46
00:02:20,233 --> 00:02:22,798
and for years it stayed
a laboratory curiosity.

47
00:02:22,899 --> 00:02:27,220
American chipmakers pooled their
effort in a group called the EUV LLC,

48
00:02:27,405 --> 00:02:32,589
while in Europe the Dutch company ASML joi
ned with the German optics maker Zeiss in

49
00:02:32,589 --> 00:02:34,379
a consortium called EUCLIDES.

50
00:02:34,472 --> 00:02:36,941
It is worth pausing on
how unusual this was,

51
00:02:37,109 --> 00:02:39,522
because it was not one
company's invention.

52
00:02:39,656 --> 00:02:41,466
Researchers in Japan began it,

53
00:02:41,647 --> 00:02:44,059
American chipmakers pooled their effort,

54
00:02:44,240 --> 00:02:47,196
and a European consortium
linked ASML with Zeiss.

55
00:02:47,283 --> 00:02:49,353
Then, in two thousand and one,

56
00:02:49,547 --> 00:02:54,981
ASML assigned a small group of people and
modest resources to build a prototype EUV

57
00:02:54,981 --> 00:02:55,468
system.

58
00:02:55,508 --> 00:02:58,312
The first prototypes shipped
in two thousand and six,

59
00:02:58,471 --> 00:03:01,328
and they were still a long
way from making real chips.

60
00:03:01,437 --> 00:03:03,366
So in two thousand and twelve,

61
00:03:03,558 --> 00:03:08,829
Intel, Samsung and TSMC agreed to contribu
te to ASML's research and development,

62
00:03:09,022 --> 00:03:11,657
because they needed the machine to exist.

63
00:03:11,741 --> 00:03:15,472
Rivals sharing the cost of one machine
tells you how badly they needed it,

64
00:03:15,624 --> 00:03:17,741
and how little any of them could do alone.

65
00:03:17,823 --> 00:03:19,575
In two thousand and thirteen,

66
00:03:19,756 --> 00:03:22,474
ASML shipped its first
EUV production system,

67
00:03:22,655 --> 00:03:25,193
nearly three decades after the first idea.

68
00:03:25,288 --> 00:03:26,864
Now we can open the machine,

69
00:03:27,033 --> 00:03:31,480
and the first thing to understand is
that nature offers no easy source of this

70
00:03:31,480 --> 00:03:31,873
light.

71
00:03:31,913 --> 00:03:34,007
It is not made by any ordinary lamp,

72
00:03:34,181 --> 00:03:36,799
so the machine has to
create it from scratch,

73
00:03:36,974 --> 00:03:38,603
with a controlled explosion.

74
00:03:38,717 --> 00:03:39,717
The fuel is tin.

75
00:03:39,881 --> 00:03:42,227
The machine ejects molten tin droplets,

76
00:03:42,407 --> 00:03:44,753
each about twenty-five micrometres wide,

77
00:03:44,933 --> 00:03:48,541
at about seventy metres per
second through a vacuum chamber.

78
00:03:48,648 --> 00:03:51,577
Each droplet is a target
narrower than a human hair,

79
00:03:51,746 --> 00:03:53,718
and it has to be hit exactly right.

80
00:03:53,791 --> 00:03:57,131
The machine drops fifty thousand
of these droplets every second.

81
00:03:57,235 --> 00:03:59,576
A carbon dioxide laser hits each one,

82
00:03:59,766 --> 00:04:01,853
and the laser fires a dual pulse,

83
00:04:02,043 --> 00:04:05,775
a weaker pre-pulse first and a
stronger main pulse after it.

84
00:04:05,885 --> 00:04:07,495
The result is a hot plasma,

85
00:04:07,674 --> 00:04:12,502
and as it blazes, it gives off the extreme
ultraviolet light the whole machine

86
00:04:12,502 --> 00:04:13,575
exists to collect.

87
00:04:13,672 --> 00:04:15,273
The timing has to be perfect,

88
00:04:15,439 --> 00:04:17,095
fifty thousand times a second,

89
00:04:17,261 --> 00:04:20,242
with the laser aimed at a
target that is barely there.

90
00:04:20,359 --> 00:04:22,028
Let us slow the whole thing down,

91
00:04:22,180 --> 00:04:24,859
to see what one second looks
like inside the chamber.

92
00:04:24,947 --> 00:04:29,685
A droplet is ejected, travelling through
the vacuum at about seventy metres per

93
00:04:29,685 --> 00:04:34,017
second, and meets a laser pulse timed
to arrive at the very same instant.

94
00:04:34,099 --> 00:04:35,731
Then another droplet follows,

95
00:04:35,900 --> 00:04:38,827
and another, fifty thousand
in that single second,

96
00:04:38,996 --> 00:04:43,329
each one a separate explosion that must
happen in the same place as the last.

97
00:04:43,432 --> 00:04:45,857
And the laser is a
machine in its own right,

98
00:04:46,022 --> 00:04:48,502
with four amplifying
lasers feeding one beam.

99
00:04:48,595 --> 00:04:51,690
The driver laser, built by
the German company Trumpf,

100
00:04:51,859 --> 00:04:55,405
has more than four hundred and
fifty thousand individual parts.

101
00:04:55,497 --> 00:05:00,083
All of that, to make a flash of light
that lasts a tiny fraction of a second and

102
00:05:00,083 --> 00:05:00,907
then vanishes.

103
00:05:00,947 --> 00:05:03,328
None of this is meant
as a laboratory trick;

104
00:05:03,545 --> 00:05:06,251
the machine is built to
run inside a chip factory,

105
00:05:06,414 --> 00:05:08,417
where it has to work again and again.

106
00:05:08,500 --> 00:05:10,955
Collecting that flash
brings the next problem,

107
00:05:11,115 --> 00:05:12,930
because the light is easy to lose.

108
00:05:13,023 --> 00:05:15,811
At this wavelength, all
matter absorbs the light,

109
00:05:15,975 --> 00:05:18,983
including air, so the machine
has to run in a vacuum.

110
00:05:19,091 --> 00:05:23,391
That is also why the optical path is
built from mirrors rather than lenses.

111
00:05:23,496 --> 00:05:25,481
But a mirror is not simple here either,

112
00:05:25,633 --> 00:05:29,806
because each one still absorbs around thir
ty percent of the light that reaches it.

113
00:05:29,917 --> 00:05:31,393
To reflect anything at all,

114
00:05:31,557 --> 00:05:36,037
the mirrors are built from many alternatin
g thin layers of silicon and molybdenum.

115
00:05:36,147 --> 00:05:40,201
The layers act together like a hall
of mirrors at a microscopic scale,

116
00:05:40,375 --> 00:05:43,387
adding up many weak reflections
into one useful one.

117
00:05:43,497 --> 00:05:45,491
Even so, after a chain of mirrors,

118
00:05:45,657 --> 00:05:47,597
much of the original light is gone.

119
00:05:47,699 --> 00:05:50,093
That is why the source
has to be so violent,

120
00:05:50,256 --> 00:05:52,649
and why every watt of
light matters so much.

121
00:05:52,734 --> 00:05:54,437
Even the stencil has to change,

122
00:05:54,602 --> 00:05:57,514
and this is one of the
cleverest parts of the design.

123
00:05:57,619 --> 00:06:02,077
An ordinary mask lets light pass through
its clear parts and blocks it elsewhere,

124
00:06:02,242 --> 00:06:05,269
but at this wavelength, all
matter absorbs the light.

125
00:06:05,363 --> 00:06:06,936
So the mask is a mirror too,

126
00:06:07,105 --> 00:06:11,093
built from forty to fifty alternating
layers of silicon and molybdenum.

127
00:06:11,197 --> 00:06:14,859
The light bounces off it, carries
the pattern toward the silicon,

128
00:06:15,023 --> 00:06:19,067
and every extra reflection along the
way costs more of the precious light.

129
00:06:19,186 --> 00:06:20,596
Think about what that means:

130
00:06:20,797 --> 00:06:22,509
the machine is a chain of mirrors,

131
00:06:22,660 --> 00:06:24,926
from the flash of tin
to the disc of silicon.

132
00:06:25,023 --> 00:06:26,288
The mirrors have a second,

133
00:06:26,434 --> 00:06:28,283
quieter demand, which is smoothness.

134
00:06:28,371 --> 00:06:33,024
Zeiss polishes the mirror blanks to a surf
ace roughness of about fifty picometres,

135
00:06:33,194 --> 00:06:35,521
a fraction of the width of a single atom.

136
00:06:35,635 --> 00:06:38,327
Stretch one of these mirrors
to the area of Germany,

137
00:06:38,482 --> 00:06:41,225
and its tallest bump would
stand one millimetre high.

138
00:06:41,321 --> 00:06:46,308
The source that reports this calls Zeiss t
he only company on Earth that knows how to

139
00:06:46,308 --> 00:06:47,481
grind an EUV mirror.

140
00:06:47,572 --> 00:06:50,591
Any larger flaw would bend
the light by a tiny angle,

141
00:06:50,762 --> 00:06:54,692
and a tiny angle at this scale
becomes an error on the finished chip.

142
00:06:54,771 --> 00:06:59,584
So the machine that prints the future depe
nds on mirrors made by a single company,

143
00:06:59,760 --> 00:07:02,401
polished to a degree
that is hard to imagine.

144
00:07:02,484 --> 00:07:05,914
Put it together, and you can see
how strange the whole device is.

145
00:07:05,993 --> 00:07:07,635
There is a droplet generator,

146
00:07:07,805 --> 00:07:10,297
a laser with hundreds
of thousands of parts,

147
00:07:10,467 --> 00:07:13,753
a vacuum chamber, and mirrors
of astonishing smoothness.

148
00:07:13,853 --> 00:07:15,337
The laser comes from Trumpf,

149
00:07:15,496 --> 00:07:18,623
the mirrors from Zeiss, and
the machine itself from ASML,

150
00:07:18,782 --> 00:07:21,643
and the system works only
when every one of them does.

151
00:07:21,737 --> 00:07:27,004
Today ASML is the only company that
produces and sells EUV systems for chip

152
00:07:27,004 --> 00:07:27,819
production.

153
00:07:27,859 --> 00:07:32,439
Anyone who wants chips drawn with this
light has exactly one supplier to turn to.

154
00:07:32,531 --> 00:07:34,298
It is a rare thing in industry,

155
00:07:34,468 --> 00:07:36,861
a single point that so much can depend on.

156
00:07:36,979 --> 00:07:38,698
Yet the story does not end there,

157
00:07:38,855 --> 00:07:41,096
because the lines on
a chip keep shrinking,

158
00:07:41,252 --> 00:07:43,389
and even this light is getting too blunt.

159
00:07:43,485 --> 00:07:46,856
The next step is a machine with
a bigger opening for the light,

160
00:07:47,017 --> 00:07:49,745
measured by a number called
the numerical aperture.

161
00:07:49,823 --> 00:07:51,897
The first commercial high-NA tool,

162
00:07:52,086 --> 00:07:55,228
with a numerical aperture
of zero point five five,

163
00:07:55,417 --> 00:07:56,863
was assembled at Intel.

164
00:07:56,978 --> 00:08:01,318
Machines of this kind cost around three
hundred and sixty million dollars each.

165
00:08:01,385 --> 00:08:04,975
A larger numerical aperture lets
the machine draw finer lines,

166
00:08:05,148 --> 00:08:07,985
but it makes everything
else harder to get right.

167
00:08:08,073 --> 00:08:10,020
And there is a deeper limit waiting,

168
00:08:10,182 --> 00:08:12,563
one that no amount of
money can polish away.

169
00:08:12,659 --> 00:08:16,692
EUV lithography is particularly
sensitive to stochastic effects,

170
00:08:16,881 --> 00:08:20,599
which is a careful way of saying
that chance gets involved.

171
00:08:20,671 --> 00:08:25,021
At these scales, each tiny patch of the
pattern receives a limited number of

172
00:08:25,021 --> 00:08:29,481
particles of light, and a limited number
fluctuates from one patch to the next.

173
00:08:29,577 --> 00:08:31,279
Some patches get a little too much,

174
00:08:31,425 --> 00:08:35,413
some a little too little, and a line that
should be straight comes out slightly

175
00:08:35,413 --> 00:08:35,713
torn.

176
00:08:35,753 --> 00:08:38,646
Picture a light shower
falling on a grid of tiles:

177
00:08:38,878 --> 00:08:42,523
no two tiles ever collect exactly
the same number of raindrops.

178
00:08:42,611 --> 00:08:44,019
The more the lines shrink,

179
00:08:44,182 --> 00:08:45,969
the more that randomness matters,

180
00:08:46,131 --> 00:08:47,973
and no engineer can switch it off,

181
00:08:48,135 --> 00:08:50,951
because it is built into
the nature of light itself.

182
00:08:51,038 --> 00:08:55,104
Engineers fight this with brighter
sources and more sensitive coatings,

183
00:08:55,276 --> 00:08:56,765
but every fix has a price,

184
00:08:56,937 --> 00:08:59,858
in speed, in cost or in
some other kind of error.

185
00:08:59,975 --> 00:09:01,568
That is the real frontier,

186
00:09:01,752 --> 00:09:05,120
no longer a question of better
lenses or better lasers,

187
00:09:05,304 --> 00:09:08,305
but of how to draw cleanly
with so few particles.

188
00:09:08,382 --> 00:09:10,046
Everything we have seen so far,

189
00:09:10,207 --> 00:09:12,943
the tin, the laser, the
vacuum and the mirrors,

190
00:09:13,104 --> 00:09:14,660
is a way of buying one thing:

191
00:09:14,875 --> 00:09:16,002
a shorter wavelength.

192
00:09:16,115 --> 00:09:18,719
What makes the story
gripping is the contrast:

193
00:09:18,945 --> 00:09:21,323
a tool so delicate that picometres matter,

194
00:09:21,493 --> 00:09:23,305
built to run on a factory floor.

195
00:09:23,411 --> 00:09:27,711
Chips made with this light trace back to
a droplet of tin meeting a pulse of light.

196
00:09:27,815 --> 00:09:30,924
That is the quiet miracle
of modern manufacturing:

197
00:09:31,172 --> 00:09:35,275
it hides its strangest ideas inside
things small enough to forget.

198
00:09:35,369 --> 00:09:37,685
And it asks a good
question about progress:

199
00:09:37,900 --> 00:09:39,462
when the physics pushes back,

200
00:09:39,623 --> 00:09:42,209
how far will people go
to keep the pencil sharp?

201
00:09:42,302 --> 00:09:44,002
So the next time you hold a phone,

202
00:09:44,152 --> 00:09:45,652
think about what is inside it.

203
00:09:45,737 --> 00:09:50,592
A pattern drawn by light, shaped by
mirrors polished past what seems possible,

204
00:09:50,774 --> 00:09:54,477
powered by tin drops exploding
fifty thousand times a second.

205
00:09:54,559 --> 00:09:57,969
It took decades and a company
most people have never heard of.

206
00:09:58,077 --> 00:10:00,087
And the strangest part is that it works,

207
00:10:00,237 --> 00:10:02,397
quietly, in a factory somewhere near you.
